{"name":"Monks Robe","hex":"#704822","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"monks","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of monk"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of monk"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/monks"},{"term":"robe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A long loose outer garment, often signifying honorary stature.","The skin of an animal, especially the bison, dressed with the fur on, and used as a wrap."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To clothe; to dress.","To put on official vestments."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/robe"}],"entries":[{"title":"Monk","blocks":[{"text":"A monk is a man who is a member of a religious order and lives in a monastery. A monk usually lives his life in prayer and contemplation. The concept is ancient and can be seen in many religions, and in philosophy, across numerous cultures. Monasticism is historically rooted in the Sramana movements of ancient India, which produced the structured ascetic orders of Jainism and Buddhism. Benedict of Nursia is considered to be the originator of western monasticism."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Christus_carthusian.jpg/330px-Christus_carthusian.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monk","wikidata":"Q733786"},{"title":"Robe","blocks":[{"text":"A robe is a loose-fitting outer garment. Unlike garments described as capes or cloaks, robes usually have sleeves. The English word robe derives from Middle English robe (\"garment\"), borrowed from Old French robe, itself taken from the Frankish word *rouba, and is related to the word rob."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/LongPao.JPG/330px-LongPao.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robe","wikidata":"Q345127